course: physics
lesson: 9
title: Riding a bike: why it stays up
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A bike stays up not by holding still but by steering: whenever it starts to tip, you turn the front wheel a little into the lean and bring the wheels back under you.
explain: That is why a moving bike balances and a still one falls over, and why riding very slowly feels wobbly.
explain: Bikes are also built to help: the shape of the front fork (the two arms holding the front wheel) makes the front wheel turn into a lean by itself, so a riderless bike pushed along rolls a long way upright.
explain: Stabilisers stop the bike tipping, so you never learn this steering, which is why balance bikes with no pedals teach children faster.
explain: Gears, brakes and tyres follow simple rules too: a low gear makes hills easy, and slamming on only the front brake can throw you over the handlebars.
fact: A moving bike stays up because the rider steers into any lean and brings the wheels back under the centre of gravity.
fact: The faster you go, the smaller the steering needed, so very slow riding is wobbly.
fact: A riderless bike pushed along can roll a long way upright, because the shape and weight of the bike make it steer into a lean by itself.
fact: Scientists found that no single magic trick keeps a bike up; it is the bike's shape and weight together.
fact: Stabilisers stop the bike tipping, so children never practise steering into a lean, while balance bikes teach that steering first.
fact: Cyclists lean into a corner to balance the sideways push of the ground on their tyres; turning sharply without leaning makes you fall outwards, and leaning hard while going very slowly tips you over inwards.
fact: A low gear turns the wheel less for each turn of the pedals, so you push more gently but pedal more times up a hill; a high gear turns the wheel more for each turn, which is good for going fast on the flat.
fact: If you squeeze only the front brake hard, the front wheel stops but your body keeps going, so use both brakes and sit back on steep hills.
fact: Big wheels roll over bumps easily, and thin, smooth tyres rub the road less.
fact: Mountain bikes have wide, knobbly tyres for grip on mud, and road bikes have thin, smooth ones for speed.
fact: A bike pump gets warm because squashing air quickly warms it, and a pumped tyre is hard because many air molecules (tiny bits of air) push on the rubber.
fact: If a front wheel starts to wobble at high speed downhill, grip lightly, press a knee on the frame and slow down gently.
fact: Riding no-hands works by leaning your body, but only at a decent speed on a smooth road, and never in traffic.
exercise: Why does a moving bike stay up but a still bike fall over? | The moving rider steers the wheels back under the lean | Moving wheels become lighter and so they float above the ground | The wind holds a moving bike up | A still bike has no centre of gravity
exercise: Why is it hard to balance on a bike that goes very slowly? | At low speed you need big steering to stay up | Slow bikes are much heavier than fast ones are | Gravity pulls a lot harder on things that go slowly | Slow wheels have no grip at all on the road surface
exercise: Can a bike balance with nobody on it? | Yes, pushed along it can roll a long way upright | No, never, not for a second | Only if it is standing still | Only if the tyres are flat
exercise: What keeps a riderless bike rolling upright? | Its shape and weight make it steer into a lean | A spinning magnet hidden inside the hub of each wheel | The air pressure in the tyres | The pedals turning round by themselves
exercise: Why do balance bikes help children learn to ride? | They teach steering into a lean first | They are lighter, so they are easier to carry home | They stop the bike from ever tipping over sideways | They have bigger stabilisers than an ordinary bike
exercise: What does a child miss when riding with stabilisers? | Learning to steer into a lean | Learning to pedal | Learning to brake | Learning to sit on the saddle
exercise: Why do cyclists lean when they turn? | To balance the sideways push of the ground on the tyres | To see round the corner better and spot cars coming the other way | To make the bike lighter | To slow the bike down
exercise: What happens if you turn sharply without leaning? | You fall outwards | You fall inwards | You go faster round the bend | Nothing different happens
exercise: Why does a low gear make hills easier? | You push more gently but pedal more times | It makes the bike lighter | It makes the hill less steep | It lets the chain pull the wheel harder for free
exercise: Why is slamming on only the front brake dangerous? | The wheel stops but your body keeps going over the bars | The back wheel starts to spin faster | The brake can catch fire at once | The bike suddenly becomes heavier
exercise: Why are a bike's wheels tall? | Big wheels roll over bumps easily | Tall wheels are lighter | Tall wheels make the bike look better | Tall wheels need no air
exercise: Why do mountain bikes have fat, knobbly tyres? | For grip on mud and rough ground | To make them faster on smooth roads | To make them much lighter to carry | So that they never get any punctures
exercise: Why does a bike pump get warm? | Squashing air quickly warms it | The rubber hose rubs your hand | The tyre sends its heat back | Pumps have a heater inside
exercise: Why does a pumped tyre feel hard? | Many air molecules inside push on the rubber | The rubber freezes when pumped | The air inside becomes heavier than the metal of the frame | The tyre shrinks when it is full
exercise: What should you do if your bike wobbles downhill? | Grip lightly, press a knee on the frame and slow gently | Grip as hard as you can and pedal faster | Slam on the front brake at once | Take your hands off the handlebars
exercise: How can you steer without holding the handlebars? | By leaning your body a little | By pedalling harder on one side | By turning your head | By shouting left or right
test: My friend says a bike stays up cos the wheels spin like a top. right? | Not really, it is mostly steering and the bike's shape | Yes, it is only the spinning | Yes, that is the one magic trick | No, it is the air in the tyres
test: Riding no-hands down a busy road is fine if you're good, innit? | No, never do it in traffic | Yes, if you are fast enough | Yes, it is safer than holding on | Only on rainy days
test: You want to go fast on a flat road. Which gear? | A high gear | A low gear | No gear at all | The lowest gear
test: In a game a rider brakes hard with only the front brake on a steep hill and stops neatly. What would really happen? | The rider could fly over the handlebars | The bike would stop smoothly and safely in a second | The back wheel would lift the rider up | The bike would roll backwards
test: Which bike would be best for a muddy forest track? | A mountain bike with wide knobbly tyres | A road bike with thin, smooth racing tyres | A bike with no tyres, just the metal rims | A bike with flat tyres that bump on the rim
test: Why do people wobble so much on a bike at walking speed? | Slow riding needs bigger steering to stay up | The bike is heavier when slow | The pedals pull you sideways | Slow wheels lose all their air
test: A small wheel and a big wheel roll into the same stone. Which gets over it more easily? | The big wheel | The small wheel | Both the same | Neither can get over it
test: bro my bike fell over when i stopped at the lights. why | With no speed you cannot steer to balance | Bikes get heavier as soon as they stop moving | The traffic lights pulled it over with magnetism | Your tyres lost all of their air at once when you stopped
test: What would happen if you leaned into a bend while going very slowly? | You would tip over inwards | You would fall outwards away from the bend | You would suddenly go a lot faster | Nothing at all would happen to you
test: Why do skilled riders sit back on steep downhill slopes? | It keeps their weight from tipping over the front | It makes the hill less steep | It makes the brakes cooler | It makes them faster
test: A tyre goes flat and the rim bumps on the road. Why did it feel hard before? | Many air molecules pushed on the rubber | The rubber was frozen solid by the cold air pumped into it | The tyre was full of water | The metal inside was bigger
exam: Identify the principal means by which a cyclist maintains equilibrium upon a moving bicycle. | By steering the front wheel into any incipient lean | By keeping the body perfectly motionless upon the saddle | By the gyroscopic action of the pedals alone | By the upward pressure of the air upon the frame
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a low gear upon the ascent of a hill? | Each push is gentler, but a greater number of revolutions is required | Each push is harder, but fewer revolutions are required | The incline of the hill is effectively diminished | The mass of the bicycle is effectively diminished
exam: It is given that a cyclist applies the front brake alone and with great force. Ascertain the hazard thereby occasioned. | The rider may be projected over the handlebars | The rear wheel may be inflated beyond its limit | The frame may be rendered incapable of steering | The pedals may be caused to rotate in reverse
exam: Determine the reason for which a stationary bicycle without support falls over. | No forward motion is available by which to steer beneath the lean | Its centre of gravity is located beneath the ground | The tyres lose their air when at rest | The frame becomes heavier when stationary
